Restore a snapshot's bottom page with room for more rows.

The snapshot decoder allocates every page at the exact capacity its record
declares, which is the page's used rows. The page holding the bottom of the
screen is then full, so the first row written after a restore starts a new
page, and the old page keeps a whole pool page with spare rows it can never
use. A snapshot keeps that layout, so each snapshot, restore and write cycle
adds one page that counts against the scrollback byte limit and stays in
memory. After about 40 cycles at a 16 MiB limit the terminal drops scrollback
that a terminal fed the same bytes keeps.

Give the bottom page of the SCREEN record as many rows as a standard page of
its width holds, while it still fits in the memory it takes anyway: a
standard page, or the largest page for one enlarged past that (more styles or
graphemes). Let the page decoder accept a destination with more rows than the
record declares.

Applied by `make libghostty` to the pinned Ghostty commit (GHOSTTY_COMMIT).

